---
/*
 * Section 1 — the hero. Copy verbatim from docs/about-page.md, with one deletion
 * recorded below.
 *
 * NO CTA HERE, AND THE COPY FILE MAKES IT A BUILD NOTE. A reader who opens About is
 * verifying, not deciding — they have heard the name or read a page and want to know
 * whether the firm behind the argument is real. A button at the top interrupts exactly
 * the thing they came to do. The page closes with one, and that is the only one.
 *
 * NO ILLUSTRATION EITHER. The founder portrait in section 3 is the only image on this
 * page, by instruction, so the head carries the band on its own: heading left, subhead
 * right, both running to the container edges. That is the homepage section 2 pattern and
 * it exists for this exact problem — a left-aligned head in a 1240px container leaves a
 * third of the band empty, and empty space on a full-width band reads as something
 * failing to load rather than as air.
 *
 * "IN METRO VANCOUVER" IS DELETED FROM THE SUBHEAD. Approved: "ArkSystems is an AI
 * consulting firm in Metro Vancouver." Fredrick's instruction of Aug 25 2026 takes the
 * location line and everything like it off the site, confirmed again on Aug 26 when this
 * page was planned: metas keep Vancouver, the visible page does not. Two other places on
 * this page lost the same thing — section 3's fourth chip and section 6's location
 * paragraph, which went whole rather than half-kept. They travel together. Do not
 * restore one alone, here or on the four other pages it was removed from.
 *
 * THE TITLE AND META DESCRIPTION KEEP IT, deliberately. See the note in about.astro.
 */
---

<section class="hero">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="hero__head">
      <p class="eyebrow hero__eyebrow">About ArkSystems</p>

      <h1 class="hero__title">
        We establish where AI produces a return. Then we build it.
      </h1>

      <p class="hero__subhead">
        ArkSystems is an AI consulting firm. We work with businesses and professional
        practices that accept AI matters and need someone to establish where it belongs in
        their own operation, what capturing it is worth, and whether the build is
        justified at all.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* White, so the cream band below it lands as a change. The ladder for this page runs
   * white → cream → surface → white → warm → stage-green → olive: seven sections and no
   * two neighbours sharing a colour. */
  .hero {
    background: var(--ark-white);
    /* Deeper than the other three heroes, which run 60/76. They each carry an
     * illustration that gives them height on its own; this one is a headline and a
     * paragraph, and at the shared padding it measured 405px — the shortest section on
     * the site, which is the wrong thing for a hero to be. The space is the presence. */
    padding-block: 96px 108px;
  }

  .hero__eyebrow {
    display: block;
    margin-bottom: 22px;
  }

  /* The measure sits on the element carrying the font-size, never on a wrapper — `ch`
   * resolves against the element's own font-size, so on a wrapper it counts 16px
   * characters and cramps a display heading to a third of its width. */
  .hero__title {
    font-size: var(--ark-h1);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-h1);
    letter-spacing: -0.03em;
    max-width: 17ch;
  }

  .hero__subhead {
    margin-top: 24px;
    font-size: var(--ark-body-lg);
    line-height: var(--ark-lh-body);
    color: var(--ark-body-ink);
    max-width: 62ch;
  }

  /* Two columns: heading left, subhead right, running to the same right edge. The exact
   * complement of the centring rule in base.css — a portrait tablet centres its heading
   * and must not also be holding two columns. */
  /* THE TWO COLUMNS END TOGETHER, and getting there took two attempts worth recording.
   *
   * First build put the subhead in `grid-row: 1 / span 2`, which anchored it to the
   * EYEBROW rather than to the headline: measured at 1440, the subhead started at 147
   * and the headline at 185, so the supporting text began 38px above the thing it
   * supports. Worse, the two columns finished 107px apart — subhead bottom 279 against
   * headline bottom 386 — which left a hole under the right column that read as content
   * failing to load.
   *
   * What ships: the subhead sits in row 2 only, beside the headline and never beside the
   * eyebrow, and `align-self: end` lands its last line on the headline's last line. The
   * space that remains is at the TOP of the right column, above the subhead, which is a
   * deliberate stagger rather than a shortfall — a reader sees a headline that dominates
   * and a paragraph anchored under it, not a column that ran out.
   *
   * The terracotta rule above the subhead is what makes the stagger legible. Without it
   * the right column starts in mid-air; with it, the block has a top edge and the empty
   * space above reads as chosen. */
  @media (min-width: 901px) and (orientation: landscape) {
    .hero__head {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.1fr) minmax(0, 0.9fr);
      column-gap: 64px;
    }

    .hero__eyebrow {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 1;
    }

    .hero__title {
      grid-column: 1;
      grid-row: 2;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      grid-column: 2;
      grid-row: 2;
      align-self: end;
      margin-top: 0;
      padding-top: 26px;
      border-top: 3px solid var(--ark-terracotta);
      max-width: none;
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 900px) {
    .hero {
      padding-block: 52px 60px;
    }

    .hero__eyebrow {
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      margin-top: 18px;
      max-width: 44ch;
    }
  }

  /* Phone and any portrait screen: the heading BLOCK centres, and the text inside the
   * subhead stays left. Centred prose is harder to read, which matters more than usual on
   * a page written for readers whose first language may not be English — but a left-flush
   * block under a centred headline reads as a misalignment, so the box centres even
   * though its text does not. That is what base.css does for
   * `.section__head .section__lede`, and all four heroes now match it.
   *
   * The subhead's auto margin belongs HERE rather than in the `(max-width: 900px)` block
   * it shipped in, in the same query that centres the headline — the two can then never
   * disagree. In the old place a 1024-wide portrait tablet centred the headline and left
   * the 62ch subhead flush left beneath it, the exact fault this rule exists to prevent.
   * At 390 the block is already wider than the column, so the auto margin changes
   * nothing there. */
  @media (max-width: 900px), (orientation: portrait) {
    .hero__title,
    .hero__eyebrow {
      text-align: center;
      margin-inline: auto;
    }

    .hero__subhead {
      margin-inline: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
